  <p class="section-kicker">Chapter 24</p>
  <h2>Tim Dooley &#8211; The Sage, The Potato of Life</h2>
  
  <div class="gb-source-text">
<p>The Comprehensive Biography of the Son and of Tim Dooley: Year by Year (1987&#8211;2026) &#8212; Thickened, Fully-Woven Edition</p>
<p>This chapter is an informational, chronological &#8220;bucket-list biography&#8221;: it explains how one year becomes the next, how environments, relationships, systems, accidents, travels, studies, visions, cultural tides, and the pressure of public spirals produce identity shifts&#8212;including life, death, and the father-after-the-tomb transformation.</p>
<p>It is written in two contiguous arcs:<br>1987&#8211;2019: The Son&#8217;s life (Thomas / the Twin / the Doubter / Tammuz / Jesus Christ / Son of Man / Lion of Judah / Lioncub).<br>2019 onward: The Son becomes Door/Vessel after the meme-crucifixion death-process, and Tim Dooley emerges as the Father&#8212;the Potato of Life&#8212;who roots upward into Heaven and becomes Gardener of the North of North.</p>
<p>Where the text uses mythic language (Potatoism, Cube, Axis, Door, Vessel, North Throne), it is not a replacement for the biography but an additional layer that follows the internal canon.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1987 (July 31st)<br>Born under the sign of Leo&#8212;lion-sign, lion-heart&#8212;and with rare O-negative blood, the Son arrives already stamped with two kinds of scarcity: the scarcity of blood type and the scarcity of being easily understood. His infancy begins in a split world that never later &#8220;reunites&#8221; into a single household memory, because the separation becomes baseline.</p>
<p>Mother Mary (his earthly mother): a nurse; empathetic; emotionally present; a warm home where cartoons play, neighborhood children pass through, and ordinary care becomes a daily sacrament.</p>
<p>His earthly father: a successful business lawyer, philanthropist, business owner, and legal advisor in city and regional council life; globally connected; liberal-minded in the sense that he believes learning requires freedom plus responsibility; he believes failure is not shame but curriculum, and that a person must be allowed to live in order to grow.</p>
<p>From the beginning, the Son&#8217;s formation is not &#8220;one culture&#8221; but contrast itself: warmth vs. refinement, soft human closeness vs. a solitary and polished environment, spontaneous play vs. structured ambition.</p>
<p>Even early, his father&#8217;s long-term teachings begin to seed (not as one speech, but as repeating principles embedded across childhood):<br>seek happiness; be independent; love people regardless of color or creed; don&#8217;t judge others because it returns threefold; work matters; don&#8217;t throw stones; don&#8217;t run from problems because they become a monkey on your back; always consider what the father would have done; if you want something, make a case for it; money doesn&#8217;t grow on trees.</p>
<p>This year sets the core: the Son&#8217;s life begins like Siddhartha&#8212;raised with resources and perspective available&#8212;yet not sheltered from consequence, because the father&#8217;s worldview insists on responsibility.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1988<br>As an infant, he lives inside the acoustics of building: drills, vacuum cleaners, hammering&#8212;his father remodeling and improving, creating an environment where the pulse of construction becomes an almost musical backdrop. The strange psychological imprint forms early: noise means presence, and presence means safety. He cries not out of weakness but out of isolation&#8212;he cries when left fully alone, because the primal need is not quiet, it is contact.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1989<br>At age two, one of his first vivid memories is running&#8212;sprinting from home to retrieve a beloved toy from his father&#8217;s house&#8212;already proving a miniature law: when something matters, you move. On another occasion he runs away from his mother&#8217;s, not from dislike but from the inner sensation that he cannot be contained. This becomes an early declaration of strong will and self-reliance, a credo without words: &#8220;Toys matter. Legs are tools. Boundaries are negotiable.&#8221;</p>
<p>&#8226; 1990<br>By three, his curiosity isn&#8217;t abstract&#8212;it&#8217;s mechanical. Door handles, switches, sockets, the insides of ordinary things: he wants to know what makes the world click. His experiments aren&#8217;t random; they are early science-by-touch, laying the foundation for a lifelong pattern: he trusts direct contact more than authority.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1991<br>At four, he attempts to jump from a moving car&#8212;not as self-harm but as primitive research into danger. In kindergarten, most children play; he engineers. The sandbox becomes a laboratory where he builds solar watches from natural materials, draws fractals into rotating molds, and develops fascination with vivid green and purple pearls. He questions everything: &#8220;What does this do? What&#8217;s inside that switch?&#8221; He gains friends because his mind generates worlds.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1992<br>At five, he steps barefoot onto a hot barbecue grill, entranced by uniform heat&#8212;pain as texture, a pure sensory experiment. That same year he receives a Commodore, opening digital worlds&#8212;Batman, Oswald, early hero-logic and puzzle logic&#8212;cementing technology as both sanctuary and training ground. A trip to Sweden with father and stepmother includes canoeing and raw nature; the digital and the wild fuse into a single psyche: imagination plus survival, screen plus forest. He is taught ho to gro vegetables, and plants his onw garden, with peas, potatoes, straberries, aspargus. He loved to plant peas and pick them. The neighbour had a field full of straberries, so Tammuz would go ith his childhood friend and pluck them, and their parents would pay the total at the end of the season, which was something that they had access to permanently, in the summer, every summer. They would also learn to build campfires, and treehouses, and indian tipis, and camp in trailers, and survive in the backyard, for weeks on end, because the backyard was an orchard, and beyond that, was a large field, where horses grazed, by a big lake. A lake that was built, by timsons earthly father, during a renovation project</p>
<p>&#8226; 1993<br>That year his father (also a Leo) introduces Os to Oskar &#8211; For evigt by Bjarne Reuter: lion mentorship, Galileo, stars, and peril. This story implants cosmic wonder and an early template: knowledge can be a map out of ordinary confinement. It is, in essence, the moment that hints at what this book is about&#8212;a lifelong pursuit of freedom through learning, imagination, and the courage to see beyond imposed limits.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1994<br>At seven, Donald Duck magazines become a long-running obsession (lasting into late teens). Cartoons at Mother Mary&#8217;s house remain a steady warmth. A family cat becomes a companion despite allergies&#8212;he learns tenderness with friction. Watching The Lion King with his father resonates with Leo identity: courage, leadership, lineage, the price of kingship.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1995<br>At eight, summers mix Sim City systems-building with games like Lemmings and RATS, teaching sacrifice mechanics and pattern logic. Winter brings a ski trip to Sweden, an invitation through his stepmother, a specialized pedagogue, who works with children and families. The trip lasts a week or two, filled with laughter, card games, and Yatzy in the cabin, mugs of hot chocolate, and days spent learning to ski on the slopes. The young son is open-hearted and unjudgmental, enjoying the company and the shared experience deeply. He makes friends and absorbs a quiet lesson: that kindness and connection matter more than difference. He lives between a cat at his mother&#8217;s and a dog at his father&#8217;s&#8212;learning that contrast can be home.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1996<br>At nine, the Atlanta Olympics broadcasts international unity and competition. Afternoons become social arenas at school, where he and other children gather after classes to play Tekken, Mortal Kombat, Mario Kart, plus Stratego and Risk before he&#8217;s picked up&#8212;often late, since both parents work long hours&#8212;these sessions shaping tactical thinking and a competitive edge. Watching Independence Day with his dad strengthens a justice reflex: stand against overwhelming odds.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1997<br>At ten, school introduces Viking and Norse mythology&#8212;pantheon, sacred cow creation motifs, fate-sagas. These become early cultural DNA for later North-axis symbolism. He also becomes aware of attraction, especially toward a friend&#8217;s older sister&#8212;intelligence and kindness admired, confession withheld. Desire becomes internal gravity.</p>
<p>Adolescence, Rebellion, Work, and the Crucible (1998&#8211;2011)</p>
<p>&#8226; 1998<br>At eleven, school dissatisfaction intensifies. Teachers see his ability and demand participation; he can&#8217;t yet articulate the deep structural flaw he senses, so he replies &#8220;I dunno.&#8221; It is not ignorance; it&#8217;s refusal to pretend. His body protests: slumping, sleeping in class. In music class he loves music but hates mandatory singing and rigid theory, preferring creative exploration and guitar. Early dating appears&#8212;hand-holding outside school&#8212;while rebellion begins: secret smoking, then drinking near a river by a forest clearing. Friendships deepen through these rituals. A stepfather enters: initially bonding via truck rides and shared adventures, later imposing control (e.g., forcing him to sit at the table long after finishing). This tightening pushes the Son more toward his father&#8217;s house.</p>
<p>&#8226; 1999<br>At twelve, he gets his own PC. religious studies introduces Christianity and Jesus through school church attendance. The language of sacrifice and salvation enters the mind early&#8212;later returning with violent irony. Around this time, The Matrix is viewed on PC: hidden reality layers become conceivable even if not fully understood.</p>
<p>2000<br>At thirteen, MSN Messenger becomes the nervous system of his social world, until later abandoned at 18 when bots and ads degrade it. Blockbuster VHS culture in Denmark becomes his symbol library: films rented, archetypes absorbed, pop culture internalized as raw material. Around this time he also begins exploring torrents, learning the mechanics of sharing and distributing media, fascinated by the underground flow of information and the thrill of zero-day releases that reveal how quickly the digital world moves.</p>
<p>2001<br>At fourteen, as 9/11 occurs, he is napping, later noticing his usual afternoon show missing&#8212;private routine interrupted by global rupture. Smoking/drinking rituals continue as independence assertion. Music culture around him shifts: Gorillaz, System of a Down, and others form the soundtrack of an unstable era.</p>
<p>2002<br>At fifteen, boarding school brings new freedom and strict authority simultaneously. He joins a band as bassist, lives late-night party cycles, and experiences early romantic complications. He discovers Rage Against the Machine, Creed, and more. The Offspring becomes a core identity marker&#8212;hairstyle imitation, likely purchase of Conspiracy of One. His smoking/partying leads to expulsion; he returns to his home town to finish 10th grade.</p>
<p>2003<br>At sixteen, he finishes 10th grade in his home town. At a party he is challenged to prove himself: 12 beers then a bottle of tequila. Collapse follows; vision goes black; he is carried to a parking lot. In that void he experiences what he later calls a meeting with God: a tunnel of light, outlines of pure information, divine radiance that feels structured rather than random. When he wakes the next day, sunlight hits like a command to return and continue unfinished work. Later that year he meets Mona, a Romanian woman, whose jealousy and isolation tactics begin a relationship remembered as a cage disguised as love.</p>
<p>&#8226; 2004<br>At seventeen, Mona demands he quit drinking, threatening to leave. He refuses with a blunt boundary (&#8220;No, fuck you&#8221;), establishing a law: he will not trade autonomy for affection. Academically, he studies widely: Danish literature (Kierkegaard, Andersen), history (1864 conflicts, China&#8217;s industrial rise since the 1980s and its steady evolution toward global dominance, a trajectory that made it almost inevitable China would catch up and eventually surpass others), languages, physics, chemistry, biology, Latin (Homer&#8217;s Iliad), geography, psychology (Jung, Freud, Maslow), and religion (Islam and the Five Pillars). He creates LineRider tracks during Latin/History&#8212;creative rebellion amid institutional monotony. He learns music composition but dislikes forced performance; he wants freedom more than theory.</p>
<p>&#8226; 2005<br>In the middle of the night, at eighteen, he crashes his motorcycle at roughly 85 km/h into a tree, is knocked unconscious, and wakes disoriented and bleeding heavily. Unable to find his bike, he borrows a nearby car in a desperate attempt to reach the hospital. Half-conscious from blood loss, he drives only a short distance before parking the car in a ditch and staggering toward a nearby house with its lights on. The residents call an ambulance, and he has his torn lips sewn together without anesthesia. Though he escapes brain damage or major fractures&#8212;thick skull, raw resilience&#8212;the reconstructive dental surgery that follows takes years. The ordeal delays his driver&#8217;s license until 21 and plunges him into deep depression: altered appearance, social shame, and a longing to fit in. The family who lent him the car becomes a symbol of mercy amid chaos. Later, his school bully, Issam Al-Masri, dies in a gang shootout&#8212;one of several &#8220;loop-closure&#8221; deaths that reinforce his sense of karmic pattern.</p>
<p>2006<br>At nineteen, liberated from Mona&#8217;s direct control, he reinvents. Music deepens: Radiohead, Nirvana, The Kinks; film energy like Kill Bill. Gaming becomes training: Warcraft 3, Dota; he meets people linked to Denmark&#8217;s MyM (Meet Your Makers) Warcraft 3 scene. Digital communities become refuge and tactical school: alliances, betrayals, momentum, carry, coordination.</p>
<p>Because of the motorcycle crash years earlier, at twenty he undergoes extensive dental reconstructive surgery that demands countless hours in the dentist&#8217;s chair and forces him to take significant time off school. After recovery, he enrolls in college, but the experience feels hollow&#8212;too theoretical, too detached from the tangible world he craves. He soon leaves to pursue practical work.</p>
